From 7837d9488bf0c5d627fc3b4259a48b1474f44bf6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mudb0y <stasprzecinek@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:52:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] Silence what a cancel overtook in the engine's callback

The callback looks at _discarding and then feeds the player. A cancel
from NVDA's thread between the two sets _discarding and stops the
player, and the feed that follows finds a stopped player, finishes the
stop and starts it again with the buffer it was given, as NVDA's
WASAPI player is written to do. Up to 4096 bytes of the cancelled
utterance, 186 ms at 11,025 Hz, then played after the reader had asked
for silence, and the player was left running.

The window is narrow and natural timing seldom hits it. Held open in
NVDA's process on Linux, by stopping the engine's thread between the
look and the feed and cancelling there, every one of 10 cancels was
followed by 136 to 186 ms of the cancelled sentence. The player is the
same on Windows, so the window is there too.

After a feed, _flush now stops the player again if a cancel came
meanwhile. A cancel that came first has made _discarding true before
its own stop, so whichever way the two interleave the buffer is
silenced, and with the window held open no more than a millisecond of
it was heard in any of the 10.

The same window lies in front of a mark with no audio in hand ahead of
it, which _flush reports at once rather than feeding: a cancel there
had the silenced utterance's mark reported straight after the cancel,
in every one of 10 tries held open the same way. NVDA's speech manager
drops an index it does not know, so speech went on, but
synthIndexReached fired for speech the reader had silenced. Such a mark
is now dropped once a cancel has come. That is the same on Windows.

nvda/test/engine.py holds both, with a player whose feed a cancel
overtakes and a flush a cancel overtakes at a mark.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 nvda/addon/synthDrivers/_openevv.py | 15 +++++++++++--
 nvda/test/engine.py                 | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 46 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/nvda/addon/synthDrivers/_openevv.py b/nvda/addon/synthDrivers/_openevv.py
index 145a4bc..fce6338 100644
--- a/nvda/addon/synthDrivers/_openevv.py
+++ b/nvda/addon/synthDrivers/_openevv.py
@@ -1253,10 +1253,21 @@ class Engine:
 				self._report(marks)
 			finally:
 				self._leftPlayer()
+			# A cancel that came after the callback looked at _discarding and
+			# before this feed stopped the player first, and a feed after a stop
+			# starts it again with the buffer it is given. Stopping it once more
+			# silences that buffer.
+			if self._discarding:
+				self.player.stop()
 		else:
 			# A mark with no audio in front of it still has to be reported, or
-			# whatever is waiting on it waits for ever.
-			self._reportIndexes()
+			# whatever is waiting on it waits for ever. Not after a cancel that
+			# came since the callback looked at _discarding, though: the mark
+			# is then one of the silenced utterance's, and nothing waits on it.
+			if self._discarding:
+				self._pendingIndexes = []
+			else:
+				self._reportIndexes()
 
 		# Waiting for the end of the audio has to happen whether or not there
 		# was anything left to hand over: an utterance ending on a mark leaves
diff --git a/nvda/test/engine.py b/nvda/test/engine.py
index 4122a31..644168f 100644
--- a/nvda/test/engine.py
+++ b/nvda/test/engine.py
@@ -872,6 +872,39 @@ def main():
     engine._discarding = False
     check("a cancel in the last wait is not followed by a done", marks, [11])
 
+    # A cancel between the callback's look at _discarding and its feed: the
+    # player was stopped before the buffer went in, and the feed started it
+    # again, so the buffer has to be stopped once it is in.
+    def feedOvertaken(data, size=None, onDone=None):
+        engine._discarding = True
+        FakePlayer.feed(player, data, size, onDone)
+
+    stopped = player.stopped
+    player.feed = feedOvertaken
+    pending[:] = [lambda: waveform(mod.FRAME)]
+    engine.synthesize()
+    del player.feed
+    engine._discarding = False
+    check("a buffer a cancel overtook is stopped once it is in",
+          player.stopped - stopped, 1)
+
+    # The same window at a mark with nothing in front of it, which is
+    # reported rather than fed: it is the silenced utterance's mark, and
+    # reporting it tells NVDA that speech it cancelled was reached.
+    def flushOvertaken(last=False):
+        if not last:
+            engine._discarding = True
+        return mod.Engine._flush(engine, last)
+
+    engine._resume()
+    marks.clear()
+    engine._flush = flushOvertaken
+    pending[:] = [lambda: index(31), lambda: waveform(20)]
+    engine.synthesize()
+    del engine._flush
+    engine._resume()
+    check("a mark a cancel overtook is not reported", marks, [])
+
     # ---- cancelling, which is what crashed NVDA when it was done properly --
 
     # cancel() is the one method meant to be called from another thread, and it
